## Layout

Every domain owns a folder with its commands, events, queries, services,
states, views and types:

```text
src/
  domains/
    <domain>/
      commands.rs
      events.rs
      queries.rs
      services.rs
      states.rs
      types.rs
      views.rs         # only when the spec declares views
      mod.rs
```

Outside the domains:

- `actors` — actors and their permissions.
- `action` — the `Action` trait implemented by every command input and query
  params struct, returning the permission that `Actor` checks.
- `domain_event` — the `DomainEvent` trait implemented by every event.
- `shared` — items that were not assigned to a domain, when there are any.

Service methods return `impl Future<Output = ...> + Send`; implement them with
`async fn`. The traits are not object-safe.

## States and views

States and views share a shape: each struct holds both the params used to load
it and its projected fields. Params are the leading fields of the struct, so
there is no separate params type.

Every projection gets an associated factory that fills the params and leaves
the projected fields at `Default::default()`:

```rust
let state = YourState::new(account_id);
```

Event application is generated as one trait with an `on_*` method per event
type the projection reads:

- **States** are in-memory decision models that are rebuilt on every
  rehydration. Their `Reducer` trait methods are synchronous and fold an event
  into the state.
- **Views** are persisted read models. Their `Store` trait methods are
  asynchronous (`impl Future + Send`) and may write the updated view back to the
  database.

A domain gets a `views.rs` module only when it declares View cards; specs
without views do not emit an empty module.

The event types and tags that feed a projection are recorded in its doc
comments.

Every file in this crate starts with a header stating that it was generated by
`em-codegen` and must not be edited by hand. Change the specification or the
templates and regenerate instead.

## Domain events

There is no central event enum. Each event struct implements the
`DomainEvent` trait, which knows its type name and tags and can produce a
stored event:

```rust
use {{ package_name }}::{DomainEvent, Event};

fn persist<E: DomainEvent>(event: &E) -> Result<Event, serde_json::Error> {
    event.to_store_event()
}
```

